{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by Aaron sorkin. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaire\" by ben Mezrich, which tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the subsequent legal battles that ensued.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and chief executive of facebook. the supporting cast includes andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, joshua jackson as the winklevoss twins, armie hammer as cambridge university student and fellow facebook co-founder, justin timberlake as sean parker, and max minghella as divya narayanan.\n\nthe film's narrative is structured around the lawsuit filed by the winklevoss twins against mark zuckerberg and harvard university. the story is presented in a non-linear fashion, jumping back and forth between different time periods and events. this non-linear structure adds to the complexity and depth of the story, making it more engaging and thought-provoking.\n\njoshua jackson's portrayal of the winklevoss twins is particularly noteworthy. he captures the essence of the twins' characters, conveying their arrogance, entitlement, and sense of injustice. andrew garfield, on the other hand, brings a certain vulnerability to the character of eduardo saverin, highlighting the complexities of his relationship with mark zuckerberg.\n\njesse eisenberg's performance as mark zuckerberg is a highlight of the film. he perfectly captures the essence of zuckerberg's character, conveying his intelligence, ambition, and emotional detachment. eisenberg's portrayal is so convincing that it's often difficult to distinguish between the actor and the character.\n\nthe film's script, written by aaron sorkin, is a masterclass in storytelling. sorkin's dialogue is witty, engaging, and insightful, providing a 